===Verb===
{{en-verb|understands|understanding|understood}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} {{lb|en|of [[communication]] or means of communication: words, statements, signs, etc.}} To [[know]] the [[meaning]] of; to [[parse]] or have parsed correctly; to [[comprehend]].
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|chapter=Prologue|passage=Thus, when he drew up instructions in lawyer language, he expressed the important words by an initial, a medial, or a final consonant, and made scratches for all the words between; his clerks, however, '''understood''' him very well.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1950|author=w:L. Ron Hubbard|title={{w|Dianetics: The Modern Science of Mental Health|Dianetics}}|url=https://archive.org/details/dianeticspowerof0000hubb/|publisher=w:New Era Publications|year_published=1999|ISBN=1-903820-14-6|OCLC=59128428|page=ix|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dianeticspowerof0000hubb/page/n10/|text=In reading this book, be very certain you never go past a word you do not fully '''understand'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal |en |date=2013-06-14 |author=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/profile/sam-leith Sam Leith]
|volume=189 |issue=1 |page=37 |magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/books/2013/may/23/holy-shit-history-swearing-mohr Where the Profound Meets the Profane]
|passage=Swearing doesn't just mean what we now '''understand''' by "dirty words". It is entwined, in social and linguistic history, with the other sort of swearing: vows and oaths.}}
#: {{ux|en|Can you repeat what you just said? I didn't '''understand'''.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} {{lb|en|generally}} To know the meaning of.
##* {{RQ:Fry Liar|page=14|passage=‘{{...|nospace=1}} I came back here, had a [[wank]] and finished that book.’<br>‘''{{w|Naked Lunch|The Naked Lunch}}''?’<br>‘Yeah.’<br>‘What did you reckon?’<br>‘[[crap|Crap]].’<br>‘You’re just saying that because you didn’t '''understand''' it,’ said Adrian.<br>‘I’m just saying that because I did '''understand''' it,’ said Tom. ‘[[any road up|Any road up]], we’d better start making some toast.{{nb...}}’}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} {{lb|en|of a skill, task, profession, etc.}}  To be thoroughly familiar with; to be able to [[undertake]] [[properly]].
#: {{ux|en|The students '''understood''' the assignment.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|usually with clause as object}} To [[comprehend]] a [[fact]] or [[principle]]; to regard or come to regard a [[belief]] as such.
#: {{ux|en|I '''understand''' that company policy says I can't get a refund, but can you make an exception?}}
#: {{ux|en|There's been no formal declaration, but it's '''understood''' that a state of war exists between the two countries}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} {{lb|en|of people}} To [[know]] the [[intent]], [[motives]] or [[character]] of; {{lb|en|of events}} to know the causes of or reasons for.
#: {{ux|en|One day you say you love me, the next you ignore me—I don't '''understand''' you!}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[believe]] or [[infer]], to [[think]] one grasps [[sufficiently]] despite potentially incomplete [[knowledge]].
#: {{ux|en|I '''understand''' that you have a package for me?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|grammar}} To [[regard]] as present when not.
#: {{ux|en|In the imperative mood, the word “you” is usually '''understood'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=Foreword|passage=&lsquo;I '''understand''' that the district was considered a sort of sanctuary,&rsquo; the Chief was saying.}}
# {{lb|en|humorous|rare|obsolete|except in|circus|acrobatics}} To [[stand]] [[underneath]], to [[support]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|2|5|passage=LAUNE: Ay, and what I do too: look thee, I'll but lean, and my staff '''understands''' me.<br>SPEED: It stands under thee, indeed.<br>LAUNCE:Why, stand-under and under-stand is all one.}}

====Usage notes====
* In its sense of "imputing meaning", use is usually limited to the past participle ''understood''.
* The obsolete perfect form ''[[understanded]]'' is occasionally found, e.g. in the ''{{w|Book of Common Prayer}}'' and the ''{{w|39 Articles}} of the Anglican Church''.

